To achieve this, you can use Google Apps Script with the Google Maps API. First, enable the Google Maps API for your project, then create a script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns. Here's an example:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue(); // Get the place name from the first cell

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ address: placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == "OK") {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(latitude); // Store the latitude in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(longitude); // Store the longitude in column C
    } else {
      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue("Error"); // Set an error value in column B if the geocoding fails
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue("Error"); // Set an error value in column C if the geocoding fails
    }
  });
}
```

Replace `"A2:A"` with the range of cells containing the place names you want to search. This script will search for the place name in the first cell of the range and store the latitude and longitude in the second and third cells, respectively. If the geocoding fails, it will set an error value in those cells.

To run the script, click on "Extensions" > "Apps Script" in the Google Sheets menu, then click on "Run" or press `Ctrl+R` (Windows) or `Cmd+R` (Mac).

