To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` class and the `GetClaim()` method to extract the claim with the desired name. Here's an example:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace WebApiJwtExample.Controllers
{
    [Route("api/[controller]")]
    [ApiController]
    public class ValuesController : ControllerBase
    {
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult Get()
        {
            var userName = GetUserNameFromToken();
            return Ok(new { message = $"Hello, {userName}!" });
        }

        private string GetUserNameFromToken()
        {
            var token = Request.Headers["Authorization"].FirstOrDefault();
            if (token == null)
            {
                throw new InvalidOperationException("No token found in the request");
            }

            var claimsPrincipal = new ClaimsPrincipal(new ClaimsIdentity(new[] { new Claim(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier, token) }));
            var userNameClaim = claimsPrincipal.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier);
            if (userNameClaim == null || string.IsNullOrEmpty(userNameClaim.Value))
            {
                throw new InvalidOperationException("No user name found in the token");
            }

            return userNameClaim.Value;
        }
    }
}
```
Make sure to install the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization` package if you haven't already. This example assumes that the token contains a claim with the `ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier` type, which represents the user's name or username. You may need to adjust the code to match your specific token structure.

Inner and outer functions are terms used in the context of functional programming. Inner functions are functions that are defined within another function, while outer functions are functions that are defined outside of any other function.

Inner functions can access the variables and parameters of their outer function, but they cannot be called directly from outside the outer function. Outer functions can be called directly from outside the function, and they can access the variables and parameters of their inner functions if they are declared with the `let` or `var` keyword.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference between inner and outer functions:
```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  let outerVariable = "outer";

  function innerFunction() {
    console.log(outerVariable); // outputs "outer"
  }

  return innerFunction;
}

const innerFunction = outerFunction();
innerFunction(); // outputs "outer"
```
In this example, `outerFunction` is an outer function that defines an inner function `innerFunction`. The `innerFunction` has access to the `outerVariable` defined in the `outerFunction`, and it can modify its value. The `outerFunction` returns the `innerFunction`, which can then be called directly from outside the `outerFunction`.

