SQL is a relational database language, and it doesn't provide functionality for creating reminders or performing actions outside of the database. However, you can use SQL to schedule a job that will check your work sessions and prompt you to take a break if necessary. You can use a scheduling tool like cron (for Unix-based systems) or Task Scheduler (for Windows) to run this job at regular intervals.

Here's an example of a cron job that checks your work sessions every minute and prompts you to take a break if 10 minutes have passed since the start time:
```bash
#!/bin/bash

while true; do
    sqlite3 your_database.db "SELECT * FROM work_sessions WHERE start_time IS NOT NULL AND end_time > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;";
    current_session=$(sqlite3 your_database.db "SELECT * FROM work_sessions WHERE start_time IS NOT NULL AND end_time > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ORDER BY start_time DESC LIMIT 1;");

    if [ -n "$current_session" ]; then
        start_time=$(echo $current_session | awk '{print $2}');
        end_time=$(echo $current_session | awk '{print $3}');
        elapsed_time=$(date --date="$end_time" +%s) - $(date --date="$start_time" +%s);

        if (( elapsed_time >= 600 )); then
            echo "It's time to take a break!"
        fi
    fi

    sleep 1;
done
```
This script will continuously check your work sessions and prompt you to take a break if 10 minutes have passed since the start time. You can adjust the interval (in seconds) between checks by changing the `sleep` command.

Note that this solution assumes that you have a SQLite database file named "your_database.db" and that your work sessions are stored in a table named "work_sessions". You may need to modify the script to fit your specific database and table names.

