To call a PowerShell script from within Terraform and allow for Read-Host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `my_script.ps1`) with your Read-Host input logic.
2. In your Terraform configuration file (`.tf`), add a `local-exec` provisioner that calls the PowerShell script:
```hcl
provider "null" {
  version = "~> 3.0"
}

resource "null_resource" "example" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File my_script.ps1"
  }
}
```
This will run the PowerShell script whenever the Terraform configuration is applied or updated. The `local-exec` provisioner will execute the command in the local shell, allowing for Read-Host input.

Note that the `null` provider is used here just to demonstrate how to use the `local-exec` provisioner. You can replace the `null` provider with any other provider that suits your needs.

To call a PowerShell script from within Terraform and allow for Read-Host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

```hcl
resource "azurerm_virtual_machine" "example" {
  # ...

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File script.ps1"
    args = ["argument1", "argument2"]

    environment = {
      VARIABLE1 = "value1"
      VARIABLE2 = "value2"
    }
  }
}
```

In this example, we're using the `local-exec` provisioner to run a PowerShell script called `script.ps1`. The `command` argument specifies the PowerShell command to execute, and the `args` argument specifies any additional arguments to pass to the script.

The `environment` variable allows us to set environment variables that can be accessed within the PowerShell script. In this case, we're setting two environment variables called `VARIABLE1` and `VARIABLE2`.

Note that the `local-exec` provisioner runs on the local machine where Terraform is installed, not on the Azure virtual machine. Therefore, the PowerShell script must be located on the local machine or accessible via a network share.

Also, keep in mind that the `local-exec` provisioner is only supported in Terraform versions 0.13 and later. If you're using an older version of Terraform, you may need to use a different provisioner, such as `file` or `remote-exec`, to run the PowerShell script.

To call a PowerShell script from Terraform that allows read-host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

```hcl
# main.tf

provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}

resource "azurerm_storage_account" "example" {
  name                     = "example-storage-account"
  resource_group_name      = azurerm_resource_group.example.name
  location                 = azurerm_resource_group.example.location
  account_tier             = "Standard"
  account_replication_type = "LRS"
}

resource "azurerm_resource_group" "example" {
  name     = "example-resource-group"
  location = "eastus"
}

resource "null_resource" "example" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -File script.ps1"
  }
}
```

In this example, we have a `null_resource` that runs a PowerShell script called `script.ps1`. The `local-exec` provisioner is used to execute the script on the local machine.

The `script.ps1` file should contain the PowerShell code that you want to run. It can use the `Read-Host` cmdlet to prompt for input.

Here's an example of how you can use `Read-Host` in the `script.ps1` file:

```powershell
# script.ps1

$input = Read-Host "Enter some input:"

Write-Host "You entered: $input"
```

When you run the `terraform apply` command, the `null_resource` will be created and the `script.ps1` file will be executed. The `Read-Host` cmdlet will prompt for input, and the output will be displayed in the console.

