Auth0 SSO (Single Sign-On) can be used to manage authentication for multiple React apps. To set this up, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Sign up for an Auth0 account and create a new application.
2. In the Auth0 dashboard, navigate to the "Applications" tab and select your newly created application.
3. Click on the "Settings" tab and copy the "Domain" and "Client ID" values.
4. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-react` library using npm or yarn.
5. Create a new file named `auth0-config.js` in each app and add the following code:
```javascript
import auth0 from '@auth0/auth0-react';

export default auth0({
  domain: 'YOUR_DOMAIN',
  clientId: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
  redirectUri: window.location.origin + '/callback',
});
```
Replace `YOUR_DOMAIN` and `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` with the values you copied from the Auth0 dashboard.
6. In each app, import the `auth0-config.js` file and use it to authenticate users. For example:
```javascript
import auth0 from './auth0-config';

function App() {
  const [authenticated, setAuthenticated] = useState(false);

  async function handleLogin() {
    try {
      await auth0.login();
      setAuthenticated(true);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {authenticated ? (
        <div>You are logged in!</div>
      ) : (
        <button onClick={handleLogin}>Log in</button>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```
With these steps, you should now have Auth0 SSO set up for multiple React apps. Users will only need to authenticate once and will be able to access all connected apps.

To set up Auth0 SSO (Single Sign-On) for multiple React apps, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Create an Auth0 account and set up your first application.
2. In your first application, enable the "Allow users to sign up and log in" option.
3. Configure the authentication settings for your first application, such as the login page URL and the callback URL.
4. In your second React app, create a new component to handle the authentication flow.
5. In the authentication component, use the `Auth0` library to initialize the Auth0 client with the configuration from your first application.
6. Use the `Auth0` library to redirect the user to the login page of your first application.
7. When the user logs in, they will be redirected back to your second React app with an access token.
8. Use the access token to authenticate the user in your second React app.
9. Repeat steps 5-8 for each additional React app that you want to support with Auth0 SSO.

Here's some sample code to get you started:
```javascript
// auth0-client.js
import { Auth0 } from 'auth0-js';

const auth0 = new Auth0({
  domain: 'your-domain.com',
  clientId: 'your-client-id',
  responseType: 'token id_token',
  scope: 'openid profile email',
  redirectUri: 'http://localhost:3000/callback'
});

export default auth0;
```
```javascript
// LoginComponent.js
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import auth0 from './auth0-client';

const LoginComponent = () => {
  const [isAuthenticated, setIsAuthenticated] = useState(false);

  const handleLogin = async () => {
    try {
      await auth0.login();
      setIsAuthenticated(true);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error logging in:', error);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {!isAuthenticated && (
        <button onClick={handleLogin}>Log in</button>
      )}

