To configure your Chrome extension to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, you can use the `chrome.tabs` API to create a content script that will run in the context of the visited page. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a new file called `inject.js` in your Chrome extension folder (usually `chrome/extensions/your_extension_id/`). This file will contain the content script code.
2. Add the following code to `inject.js`:
```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = 'path/to/your/javascript/file.js';
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```
Replace `'path/to/your/javascript/file.js'` with the actual path to your JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject into the visited page.
3. In your Chrome extension manifest file (`manifest.json`), add the following code to the `content_scripts` section:
```json
{
  "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
  "js": ["inject.js"]
}
```
This will tell Chrome to inject the `inject.js` file into all visited pages.
4. Save both files and reload your Chrome browser to test your extension. The JavaScript file should now be injected into the visited pages.

Note: Make sure to follow Chrome's extension guidelines and best practices when developing your extension.

To inject JavaScript into an existing webpage using your Chrome extension, you can use the `chrome.tabs` API to execute a script in the background page of the website. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a background script (background.js) for your extension. This script will handle the injection of JavaScript.
2. Inject the background script into the webpage using the `chrome.tabs.executeScript()` method. You can do this by adding the following code to your extension's manifest file (manifest.json):
```json
{
  "permissions": ["activeTab"],
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["background.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```
This will inject the background script into all tabs that your extension is active in.
3. In the background script (background.js), use the `chrome.tabs.query()` method to get the current tab and then use the `chrome.tabs.executeScript()` method again to inject your JavaScript code into the webpage. Here's an example:
```javascript
chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, function(tabs) {
  chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: "your_script.js"});
});
```
Replace `your_script.js` with the name of your JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject into the webpage.
4. Save your extension and test it by installing it in Chrome. The JavaScript code from your extension should now be injected into the webpage when you visit a supported URL.

