To make the generated planets less clustered, you can implement a simple space partitioning algorithm like a grid or a spatial hash. This will help you efficiently check for collisions between planets and avoid placing them too close to each other.

Here's a simple example using a 2D grid:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance, gridSize) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        const gridX = Math.floor(coord / gridSize);
        const gridY = Math.floor(p.position[index] / gridSize);

        return gridX === p.gridX && gridY === p.gridY && coord - p.position[index] < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
      planet.gridX = Math.floor(planet.position[0] / gridSize);
      planet.gridY = Math.floor(planet.position[1] / gridSize);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this example, we introduce a `gridSize` parameter that determines the size of the grid. Before checking for collisions, we first calculate the grid coordinates of each planet. If two planets are in the same grid cell and their distance is less than `minDistance`, we consider them too close and reject the planet.

You can adjust the `gridSize` parameter to control the density

